WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.734 line:0%
-I'm looking for --
-Oh!

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:02.735 line:0%
♪ Good day, miss ♪

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.370 line:0%
♪ Lookin' pretty,
lookin' lonely ♪

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.005 line:0%
♪ Looks like you could
use a child ♪

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:08.174 line:0%
♪ Or two or three ♪

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.708 line:0%
♪ For a very modest fee ♪

00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:11.677 line:0%
♪ If, by any chance,
you're searchin' ♪

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:12.945 line:0%
♪ For an urchin ♪

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:15.514 line:0%
♪ I got your merchan...dise
right here ♪

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:17.684 line:0%
♪ But I'll be clear,
tell you straight ♪

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:18.951 line:0%
♪ They're not great ♪

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:20.553 line:0%
♪ And they don't appreciate ♪

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:24.390 line:0%
♪ They're possibly
the worst brats in town ♪

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.158 line:0%
-So, you're s...elling
the orphans?

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:28.294 line:0%
-♪ And me? ♪

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.096 line:0%
♪ I'm just a woman
with needs of me own ♪

00:00:31.096 --> 00:00:33.299 line:0%
♪ Who'd like to do
some misdeeds of me own ♪

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:36.903 line:0%
[ Laughs ]
♪ With a certain someone ♪

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.204
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Hey, welcome back.

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:40.707
We're here at "Late Night"
with Kristin Chenoweth.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:43.242
That was a clip
from "Schmigadoon!"

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:46.378
Before we get to "Schmigadoon!,"
I'm going to say, real quickly,

00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:48.647
we're coming up on the
20th Anniversary of "Wicked."

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:50.216
Can you believe it?
-No.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.751
[ Cheering and applause ]
No.

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.953
-This is a role,

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.756
obviously, a lot of people
still associate you with.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:58.491
I mean, they associate you
with a lot of different things,

00:00:58.491 --> 00:00:59.893
but we were talking backstage.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.361
You only you only did "Wicked"
for nine months.

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:03.596
-Nine months.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:06.199
-That is crazy.
-In fact, like five years ago,

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:09.569
I was in CVS
on like 73rd and Broadway,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.837
and I was just doing
my own thing at 7:30,

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:13.239
getting all my crap.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.641
[ Laughter ]
And the lady comes in
and she goes,

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:17.210
"You're -- It's half hour.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.144
You need to get to the theater."

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:21.347
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:24.049
I said, "Oh, I'm not in a show
right now."

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:25.285
She goes, "Yes, you are.
You've got 'Wicked.'

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:26.785
You need to be
at the Gershwin, now."

00:01:26.785 --> 00:01:30.123
[ Laughter ]
I was like, "Don't argue
with me, lady. I know me.

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.258
[ Laughter ]
It's been 15 years."
And now, it's 20.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.359
I can't believe it.
-I can't believe it, either.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.194
-And I was only 15
when I was cast.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:36.829
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Oh, my God.
-Kidding.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:37.963
-So young.
-♪ I'm kidding ♪

00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:40.066
-Yes. So precocious at the time.
-Oh, yes.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.501
-This is such a special show.

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:43.636
It's such a special cast.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.404
But it's, you know,
it's inspired

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:47.039
by the musical -- this season,

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:48.608
by the musicals
of the '60s and '70s.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.944
But it's like
a musical variety show

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.945
and there's nothing like it.

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:54.847
I mean, it feels like
from a bygone era.

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:57.316
-It is.
And, Seth, for me,

00:01:57.316 --> 00:02:01.320
for my comrades --
I call them comrades.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.056
You know, it's Alan
and Jane Krakowski

00:02:04.056 --> 00:02:10.596
and DuBose and Dove and Jaime
and Ann Harada and all of us.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:13.032
It feels like
our "Saturday Night Live."

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:15.968
Like it feels like
a Christopher Guest thing

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.002
that put us together.

00:02:17.002 --> 00:02:19.171
We're allowed to do
our music theater

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:23.142
and sort of make fun of
and also love our milieu.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.812
And, for me, it's the best job.

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:28.181
And I just --
Cinco Paul's brain --

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.115
our writer -- it must hurt.

00:02:30.115 --> 00:02:32.151
[ Laughter ]
There's a lot of noodles
in there.

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:33.452
And this year he gave --

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:35.154
you know, last year,
I played a very buttoned up --

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:36.221
I had a --

00:02:36.221 --> 00:02:38.691
I had a five-page song

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.360
[ Cheering and applause ]
without a cut.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.462
-Yeah. A very fast...
-Patter.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.497
-...yeah, patter song.
-I mean, talk about sweat,

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.265
you know? This year
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:48.901
the role is a hybrid

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.670
of Miss Hannigan from "Annie"

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.271
and Miss Lovett
from "Sweeney Todd."

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:53.773
-I mean, those are
some pretty meaty roles.

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:56.175
-They're very meaty.
And the singing --

00:02:56.175 --> 00:02:57.543
he always gives me hard stuff.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:02:58.577
I'm like, "Really?"

00:02:58.577 --> 00:03:00.679
[ Laughter ]
But, actually, I love it.

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:05.317
It's a challenge and everybody
just knocks it out of the park.

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.319
We think that we are very lucky

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:10.355
to be doing what we love to do
on camera.

00:03:10.355 --> 00:03:12.524
-It's really fun.
Anytime you see

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:14.493
that the people making the show
love it

00:03:14.493 --> 00:03:16.328
and there are so many people,
obviously,

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:18.664
who love that world
on a granular level,

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:21.000
who can appreciate exactly
what the references are.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.668
And then there's people like me,
who just appreciate it

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:23.836
for a great performance,

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.204
which is really fun to watch.
-Thank you.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.173
That would be like my brother,
who said, "Hey, listen,

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.108
don't take me to any more
musical theater, hey.

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.543
I love ya.
I don't like it."

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.444
[ Laughter ]
But he loves the show

00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:35.748
because Keegan and Cecily
are like hilarious

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.851
and then we're all wacko
around them, so.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:42.188
[ Laughter ]
-You know what I think it shows?

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.757
Which is, even people
who do musical theater

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.459
appreciate that there are
some eyerolly tropes

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:49.194
about musical theater.
-We all can.

00:03:49.194 --> 00:03:51.364
-Right.
And so this show both has

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:53.299
the execution of wonderful songs

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:54.733
that you would see
in a musical theater

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.236
and the characters who are
standing on the outside of it,

00:03:57.236 --> 00:03:59.205
who maybe have
your brother's perspective,

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.107
which is why people
from both sides can come in

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:02.475
and enjoy the show.
-That's exactly right.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.810
I mean, when he saw last season,
"You know what?

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.647
I like, you know,
I like the show."

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.349
[ Laughter ]
I'm like, "That's a huge

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:10.917
compliment, for you, isn't it?
Thank you."

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:12.484
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.484 --> 00:04:14.053
Alan -- we've talked about your
friendship with Alan Cummings.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.287
How many years are you guys
going on,

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:17.156
as far as not just friends,
but collaborators?

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:20.492
-I want to say 26 or 27.

00:04:20.492 --> 00:04:22.161
He knows me and I
know him real well.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:24.297
In fact, I was just
on a cruise.

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.099
While it was at dock
because I don't cruise

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.666
because I have veneers.
It doesn't matter.

00:04:27.666 --> 00:04:31.137
[ Laughter ]
And I can't -- like I can't --
I have vertigo.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.939
And I was headlining this thing
and he was singing as well

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:36.742
and we just laugh and have fun.

00:04:36.742 --> 00:04:38.778
And I think we should
all count ourselves lucky

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.747
if we have one friend
that lasts that long,

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:44.584
that you work with,
that you can laugh, still.

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:47.286
I mean, we still think
we got it.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:48.420
We still think we got it.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.955
-Well, we think so as well.
-Thank you.

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:52.491
-Somebody else
who still has it,

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.527
which is incredible
and worth honoring,

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:56.896
and you did just honor,
is Carol Burnett.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:59.698
[ Cheering and applause ]
It was a special event
for Carol Burnett.

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:01.801
I had to double-check it.
Is it true?

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:04.503
Is 90 the number?

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:07.206
-She's 90.
-That is incredible!

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.642
Y'all, she is so much the same.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:11.043
She is so
[ Snapping fingers ] sharp.

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:13.112
She's been a mentor of mine
for several years.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.980
I might not want to say like 20.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.249
And when we get together,

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:17.617
it's usually not
about show business.

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.886
It's usually about romance
and life and all that stuff.

00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:22.955
And she's been there
through all the guys.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.490
[ Laughter and ohhs ]
I shouldn't --

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.858
[ Applause ]
Oops!

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.893
-Most of those are
from Vancouver, right? Yeah.

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:29.061
[ Laughter ]
-Of course!

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:30.662
Where I left them.

00:05:30.662 --> 00:05:33.166
[ Laughter ]
So, she has been
a great mentor to me

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:36.769
and she's accepted and loved me
and given me great advice.

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:39.305
But we did a tribute
to her 90th birthday

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:40.573
and all the years
in television.

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:41.974
And think about this --

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.543
so, I love her,
Bernadette Peters,

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:45.577
and Julie Andrews.
Those are my

00:05:45.577 --> 00:05:47.346
music theater queens, right?
-Whoo!

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:49.915
-Here she is sitting
by her bestie, Julie,

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.851
and then I'm singing
with Bernadette.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.953
If you want to talk
about a Mylanta moment,

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.354
[ Laughter ]
that was one.

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:58.157
-Now, can I ask?
In a moment like that,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:00.025
can you be in the moment
and enjoy it

00:06:00.025 --> 00:06:02.295
or are you just so terrified?

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:04.663
Because I feel I have
to remind myself --

00:06:04.663 --> 00:06:05.965
not that I've had a moment
quite like that --

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.900
but were are you like,
"Oh, this will only --

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.501
I will only be able
to look back on it fondly

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:10.770
if it goes well"?

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.939
And you seem, so effortlessly,
to pull those off.

00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:14.507
But do you have butterflies?

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:15.674
-Of course.

00:06:15.674 --> 00:06:17.509
Not necessarily with Carol
so much, anymore,

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:18.911
because we have spent
so much time

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:21.813
and we both like Cosmos
and anyway.

00:06:21.813 --> 00:06:23.515
[ Laughter ]
We've spent a lot.

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:25.985
But still Julie.
I mean --

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:27.586
[ As Andrews ]
♪ The hills are alive ♪

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:30.589
[ Cheering and applause ]
That's why I became a singer!

00:06:30.589 --> 00:06:31.957
-[ Laughs ]
-I love. I mean,

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:33.259
that's why I became a singer.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.360
And then there's Bernadette.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.995
So, I just always say,
"You know what?

00:06:36.995 --> 00:06:39.532
You're of an age, now, that you

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:40.799
are being invited to the table

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:42.000
to do what you do
with the greats

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.203
and just try to be
as great as you can."

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:45.338
-Well, there you go.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:46.405
It's certainly working out,
so far.

00:06:46.405 --> 00:06:47.874
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:50.542
-Thanks for having me.
-Kristin Chenoweth.

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:51.810
[ Cheering and applause ]
New episodes of "Schmigadoon!"

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.712
are available Wednesdays
on Apple TV+.

00:06:53.712 --> 00:06:56.081
"Carol Burnett:
90 Years of Laughter and Love"

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:58.217
premieres April 26th here
on NBC.

00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:00.118
You can see Kristin there
as well.

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:01.320
It's available the next day
on Peacock.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.855
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

